## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to architect heterogeneous retrieval with dynamic chunking, versioned embeddings, and streaming results. It also probes freshness, updates, and cross-doc weighting in a unified vector store.

## Key Concepts

- Heterogeneous doc types
- Per-type dynamic chunking
- Versioned embeddings and metadata
- Cross-document re-ranking
- Streaming partial results

## Code Example

```javascript
// Skeleton: per-type chunk sizes and basic chunking hook
const CHUNK_SPECS = { api: 512, policy: 1024, incident: 1024 };
function chunkDocument(doc){
  const max = CHUNK_SPECS[doc.type] ?? 512;
  // simple approach: split into sentences then group by max tokens
  const sentences = splitIntoSentences(doc.text);
  const chunks = [];
  let cur = '';
  for (const s of sentences){
    if ((cur + s).length > max){ chunks.push(cur.trim()); cur = s; }
    else { cur += s; }
  }
  if (cur) chunks.push(cur.trim());
  return chunks;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle updates to docs and avoid stale embeddings?
- What metrics and tests validate per-type chunking efficacy and latency?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes practical retrieval system design under real constraints: multi-tenant isolation, chunking strategies, vector DB selection, and observability.

## Key Concepts
- Chunking strategy: window size, overlap
- Embeddings and vector DB: model choice, dim, indexing
- Retrieval pipeline: dense + lexical fallback, reranking
- Multi-tenant concerns: RBAC, isolation, per-tenant relevance tuning
- Observability: metrics, traces, SLOs
- Update strategy: incremental indexing, versioning

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code for two-stage retriever
async function retrieve(query, tenantId){
  const blocks = chunkDocsForTenant(tenantId);
  const qVec = embed(query);
  const topDense = vectorDB.search(qVec, {k: 10, tenant: tenantId});
  const topLexical = lexicalSearch(query, tenantId);
  const merged = rerank([topDense, topLexical], query);
  return topK(merged, 5);
}
```

